---
import PrepLayout from '../layouts/PrepLayout.astro';
---

<PrepLayout
  title="Generic 2, 4, and 8-week ML interview plans"
  description="Adaptable 2-, 4-, and 8-week preparation plans for senior ML and AI interview loops."
  heading="Choose a horizon you can execute."
  lede="These plans provide cadence, not personalization. Choose based on current retrieval quality and available time—not the date you wish you had started."
>
  <section class="plan-chooser">
    <a href="#plan-2-week"><strong>2 weeks</strong><span>Consolidate an already-strong base</span></a>
    <a href="#plan-4-week"><strong>4 weeks</strong><span>Repair focused gaps and rehearse a full loop</span></a>
    <a href="#plan-8-week"><strong>8 weeks</strong><span>Build breadth, depth, and repeated retrieval</span></a>
  </section>

  <section class="callout">
    <h2>Before choosing</h2>
    <p>Take the <a href="/readiness/">browser-only readiness check</a>. Use eight weeks if three or more important areas feel weak, four weeks if the foundation exists but is inconsistent, and two weeks only when most answers are already workable under pressure.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="plan-2-week" class="plan-section">
    <header><p>Fast consolidation</p><h2>2-week plan</h2><span>Best for: an active interviewer with a strong base</span></header>
    <div class="week-grid">
      <article>
        <h3>Week 1 · Diagnose and repair</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Run two closed-book baseline questions from different rounds.</li>
          <li>Choose two technical gaps and one behavioral gap—no more.</li>
          <li>Complete 4–6 timed attempts, reading only after each attempt.</li>
          <li>Draft six story-bank outlines and rehearse two aloud.</li>
          <li>Schedule every weak attempt for a retry 48 hours later.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Exit:</strong> each likely round has at least one workable answer.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Week 2 · Integrate and taper</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Run one mixed technical session and one behavioral session.</li>
          <li>Complete one realistic full-loop simulation early in the week.</li>
          <li>Repair only failures exposed by the simulation.</li>
          <li>Review concise outlines, not full prose or new reading lists.</li>
          <li>Use the <a href="/final-week/">final-week checklist</a> for the last 72 hours.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Exit:</strong> no high-priority answer remains unattempted.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="plan-4-week" class="plan-section">
    <header><p>Focused rebuild</p><h2>4-week plan</h2><span>Best for: a solid foundation with identifiable gaps</span></header>
    <div class="week-grid">
      <article><h3>Week 1 · Baseline and foundations</h3><ul><li>Attempt one question from every expected round.</li><li>Refresh only foundational concepts that blocked an answer.</li><li>Start the story bank and identify missing evidence.</li><li>Create a retry queue: Weak in 2 days, Review in 1 week.</li></ul><p><strong>Exit:</strong> three priorities and a bounded gap list.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 2 · Domain depth</h3><ul><li>Practice the dominant product or technical domain.</li><li>Move from definitions into mechanisms, metrics, and failures.</li><li>Answer at least one follow-up a level deeper per attempt.</li><li>Reattempt Week 1 weaknesses before adding more questions.</li></ul><p><strong>Exit:</strong> one deep, follow-up-ready answer per technical domain.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 3 · Systems and evidence</h3><ul><li>Run two end-to-end design or production scenarios.</li><li>Practice coding if it is in the loop; do not substitute reading.</li><li>Rehearse four behavioral stories with skeptical follow-ups.</li><li>Mix question categories so retrieval cues are less predictable.</li></ul><p><strong>Exit:</strong> a credible answer across the whole loop.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 4 · Simulation and consolidation</h3><ul><li>Run one full-loop simulation in the first half of the week.</li><li>Repair only high-impact gaps revealed by evidence.</li><li>Complete final delayed retries.</li><li>Taper volume and protect sleep, setup, and answer structure.</li></ul><p><strong>Exit:</strong> stable retrieval, not exhausted coverage.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="plan-8-week" class="plan-section">
    <header><p>Breadth plus depth</p><h2>8-week plan</h2><span>Best for: role transitions, broad loops, or multiple weak areas</span></header>
    <div class="week-grid compact">
      <article><h3>Week 1 · Diagnose</h3><p>Baseline every expected round; choose three priorities; start six story outlines.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 2 · Fundamentals</h3><p>Repair core ML, math, or implementation gaps that block higher-level reasoning.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 3 · Domain depth</h3><p>Build mechanisms, metrics, failure modes, and trade-offs in the target domain.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 4 · System design</h3><p>Practice requirements, architecture, data, evaluation, serving, and iteration end to end.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 5 · Production and coding</h3><p>Debug realistic failures; implement under time pressure if the loop includes coding.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 6 · Behavioral depth</h3><p>Pressure-test ownership, conflict, ambiguity, impact, and leadership evidence.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 7 · Mixed simulation</h3><p>Interleave rounds, run a full loop, and turn every miss into a bounded repair task.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Week 8 · Consolidate</h3><p>Finish retries, rehearse concise outlines, taper volume, and protect interview readiness.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>The weekly operating rule</h2>
  <ol class="step-list">
    <li><strong>Attempt first:</strong> spend more time retrieving and explaining than consuming notes.</li>
    <li><strong>Repair second:</strong> study only the mechanism or evidence exposed as weak.</li>
    <li><strong>Retry later:</strong> delayed retrieval is the proof that repair worked.</li>
    <li><strong>Reserve capacity:</strong> leave roughly one block per week unscheduled for spillover or recovery.</li>
  </ol>

  <section class="callout">
    <h2>Plans are not coverage contests</h2>
    <p>A smaller set of answers that can survive follow-ups is more useful than touching every page. If a plan repeatedly exceeds your available time, reduce topics before reducing retries or simulation.</p>
  </section>
</PrepLayout>

<script>
  import { trackPublicEvent } from '../utils/publicEvents';
  trackPublicEvent('generic_plan_opened');
</script>

<style>
  .plan-chooser { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: .7rem; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .plan-chooser a { padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--c-rule); border-radius: var(--radius); color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
  .plan-chooser a:hover { border-color: var(--c-accent); }
  .plan-chooser strong, .plan-chooser span { display: block; }
  .plan-chooser strong { margin-bottom: .25rem; color: var(--c-accent); }
  .plan-chooser span { color: var(--c-text-soft); font-size: .82rem; line-height: 1.4; }
  .plan-section { padding-top: .5rem; scroll-margin-top: 5.5rem; }
  .plan-section > header { margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; padding-bottom: .9rem; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--c-text); }
  .plan-section > header p { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .68rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--c-accent); }
  .plan-section > header h2 { margin: .2rem 0 !important; font-size: 1.7rem !important; }
  .plan-section > header span { color: var(--c-text-soft); font-size: .86rem; }
  .week-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: .8rem; }
  .week-grid article { padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--c-rule); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--c-surface); break-inside: avoid; }
  .week-grid h3 { margin: 0 0 .55rem !important; }
  .week-grid ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .week-grid li { margin: .35rem 0; font-size: .9rem; }
  .week-grid article > p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; color: var(--c-text-soft); font-size: .86rem; }
  .week-grid.compact article p { margin: 0; color: var(--c-text-soft); font-size: .9rem; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) { .plan-chooser, .week-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  @media print { .plan-section { break-before: page; } }
</style>
